        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Inuit</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Arctic and Subarctic north of the tree line: Inuit Nunangat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nunavut">Nunavut</a>, Nunavik in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, Nunatsiavut in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a> and the Inuvialuit Settlement Region), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> (Kalaallit Nunaat), northern and northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> and a few communities on the Chukotka coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; Greenlandic communities also live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Thule people, ancestors of the Inuit, emerge from the Bering Strait and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> around 1000 CE and reach west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> by 1100 CE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-ice-age">Little Ice Age</a> from about 1350; contact with the Norse, then with Martin Frobisher in 1576; settlement and relocation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in the 1950s and 1960s; land claims and self-government from the 1970s to the present.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the qajaq (kayak) and the umiaq; dog sleds (qamutiik) and the Canadian Inuit Dog; the igloo and the skin tent (tupiq); the inuksuk; women’s face tattoos (kakiniit); carving, printmaking and throat singing; Inuit Qaujimajatuqangit, traditional knowledge and law; the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nunavut">Nunavut</a> in 1999</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Inuit oral tradition; the Norse sagas; the accounts of William Edward Parry and George Francis Lyon, published in 1824; the work of Franz Boas, Knud Rasmussen and Vilhjalmur Stefansson; archaeology such as the Ukkuqsi site near Utqiaġvik; genetic studies such as one published in Science in 2014; Canadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> figures.</span></div>
