    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Inuit, “the people” in Inuktitut and Kalaallisut, descend from the Thule people, who spread east from the Bering Strait around 1000 CE and replaced the Dorset culture, the Tuniit of Inuit tradition.</li><li>They lived by hunting whales, seals, caribou and other animals, travelling by qajaq, umiaq and dog sled, and shared food within the household and, to a large extent, the whole community.</li><li>Disease, missionaries and Canadian policy, including the High Arctic relocation of the 1950s, transformed Inuit life, but Inuit organised from the 1970s and won land claims, among them the agreement that created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nunavut">Nunavut</a> in 1999.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A girl of about nine, buried near Utqiaġvik in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> around 1200 CE, had been born with a congenital defect and could never walk, yet her family had cared for her all her life.</p></aside>
    </section>

